//// Decision 55 — `break <value>` in a collection loop contributes its value and
//// ends the loop. Decided 2026-09-18; written into decision 8 §10.
////
//// The same body as `run/loop_yield_then_break_value.bp` with its two statements
//// **swapped**: 10 yields 20, 20 breaks with 99 before reaching its `yield`, and
//// 30 is never visited. So the loop is `[20, 99]` — two elements.
////
//// Decision 55 is what makes the two cells differ by exactly the swap. Measured
//// at `b5a9b85d`, three of the four backends answer something that depends on the
//// textual order and none answers this:
////
////   commonJS  3  20,99,60      — the loop runs on after the break
////   beam      3  20,99,60      — the same
////   erlang    3  20,40,60      — the break is ignored outright
////   wasm      4  20,99,40,60   — the break value is appended and the loop runs on
////
//// Re-measured at `85f883bd` (2026-09-20), after C-06's wasm half (`8594e4ba`): wasm
//// answers `2` / `20,99` and its line is gone; the other three are unchanged.

pub fn main() {
    val r = for ([10, 20, 30]) { x ->
        if (x == 20) { break 99; };
        yield x * 2;
    };
    @print(r.length);
    @print(r.join(","));
}
